# The shape of the product

`ProductAst` is the shape of the product — the author's code, with each transclusion expressed as an edge rather than copied. Its unit is the `Code` of one section. The product pass builds one `Code` per section, and `fromProduct` later flattens a section to text and mappings, following its edges across the corpus.

An edge holds a target key, not a pointer. So the schema stays flat and serialisable even though the graph it describes recurses — and may hold a cycle, which `fromProduct` cuts.

Code is one of two channels. Prose is its peer, a `WovenProse` woven from the same fragments. A tangle section returns neither: it binds a composed result to a file path as a `File`, the descriptor the tangler writes.

## The product node

`productNode` builds each product node: it stamps a `type` discriminator and a defaulted `health` onto the fields it wraps. There is no render-order annotation, since the product is flattened by a graph walk rather than by emitting fields in order.

=>

const productNode = <Tag extends string, Fields extends Schema.Struct.Fields>(
  tag: Tag,
  fields: Fields,
) =>
  Schema.Struct({
    type: Schema.tag(tag),
    health: HealthSchema.pipe(Schema.withConstructorDefault(Effect.succeed(okHealth))),
    ...fields,
  })

## Section identity

A `SectionId` is a node's corpus-wide identity: the `.loom` `path` and the section `name` within it. Two files may each define a `Main`, so identity is module-qualified. `keyOf` JSON-encodes the pair into the key `fromProduct` uses for its cycle-cutting visited set.

=>

export const SectionIdSchema = Schema.Struct({
  path: Schema.String,
  name: Schema.String,
})
export type SectionId = typeof SectionIdSchema.Type

export const keyOf = (id: SectionId): string => JSON.stringify([id.path, id.name])

## The leaves

A `Fragment` is a literal product span — the code the author wrote — with the `.loom` `origin` it maps 1:1 back to.

A `Ref` is a transclusion edge. It records one name anchor, `::[Heading]`, so the projection composes the section the anchor names into the section that holds the anchor, and the two share one scope. `Ref` is the composing edge, named `NameRef`. A section's code holds one other kind of edge, the reference, which the section "A reference to a module" describes.

A `NameRef` carries five fields. Its `name` is the name the anchor writes, exactly as written. Its `target` is the `SectionId` the edge points at. The target is absent when the product pass, which reads one loom at a time, finds no section of that name in the anchor's own loom. The name may still lie elsewhere in the book, so the compiler binds it once it holds the corpus. The `anchor` field is the span of the name in the section that holds the anchor, where a diagnostic about the edge lands. `toBeSettled` is the span of the anchor's `{To be settled}` label, when the anchor carries one: it says the section the name reaches is meant, though nobody has written it yet. `provides` holds each name the anchor says its section provides, as a `Provided` with the span where the anchor writes it. Stating those names promises the section as surely as the label does, so an edge that carries either one says its section is meant, and `isMeant` asks whether it does.

=>

export const FragmentSchema = productNode('Fragment', {
  text: Schema.String,
  origin: PositionSchema,
})
export type Fragment = typeof FragmentSchema.Type

export const ProvidedSchema = Schema.Struct({
  name: Schema.String,
  at: PositionSchema,
})
export type Provided = typeof ProvidedSchema.Type

export const NameRefSchema = productNode('NameRef', {
  name: Schema.String,
  target: Schema.Option(SectionIdSchema),
  anchor: PositionSchema,
  toBeSettled: Schema.Option(PositionSchema),
  provides: Schema.Array(ProvidedSchema),
})
export type NameRef = typeof NameRefSchema.Type

export const isMeant = (ref: NameRef | ModuleRef): boolean =>
  Option.isSome(ref.toBeSettled) || Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty(ref.provides)

export const RefSchema = NameRefSchema
export type Ref = typeof RefSchema.Type

## A reference to a module

An anchor that stands where an import's path goes names a module, the file another section tangles to, and composes nothing. The product pass records such an anchor as a `ModuleRef`, a reference. Its fields follow a `NameRef`'s: the `name` as the anchor writes it, the `target` section when the anchor's own loom holds one of that name, the `anchor` span where a diagnostic lands, and the `toBeSettled` label and `provides` names that promise what the module is to hold.

Two fields are a reference's own. `file` is the `WrittenFile` the anchor writes in parentheses after its name, as `::[Room's 2d system](room.ts)` does. It picks one of the files a chapter emits, and records the file's name and the span of the parentheses, which a fix rewrites. `slot` is the empty span just past the anchor's closing bracket, where a fix writes a file the anchor did not name.

A reference carries no path. Where the module lands depends on the corpus around it, so the compiler finds the file and the language package writes the path, once both are known.

=>

export const WrittenFileSchema = Schema.Struct({
  name: Schema.String,
  at: PositionSchema,
})
export type WrittenFile = typeof WrittenFileSchema.Type

export const ModuleRefSchema = productNode('ModuleRef', {
  name: Schema.String,
  target: Schema.Option(SectionIdSchema),
  file: Schema.Option(WrittenFileSchema),
  anchor: PositionSchema,
  slot: PositionSchema,
  toBeSettled: Schema.Option(PositionSchema),
  provides: Schema.Array(ProvidedSchema),
})
export type ModuleRef = typeof ModuleRefSchema.Type

## Composed code

A `Code` is one section, composed: its `origin` identity, its product `languageId`, and its `fragments` in composition order — a transcluded section before the code that uses it. Each fragment is a `Fragment`, a literal span, a `Ref`, a transclusion edge, or a `ModuleRef`, a reference; `Part` is the union of the three. The product pass returns one `Code` per section, keyed by name.

=>

export const PartSchema = Schema.Union([FragmentSchema, RefSchema, ModuleRefSchema])
export type Part = typeof PartSchema.Type

export const CodeSchema = productNode('Code', {
  origin: SectionIdSchema,
  languageId: Schema.String,
  fragments: Schema.Array(PartSchema),
})
export type Code = typeof CodeSchema.Type

## The prose peer

A `WovenProse` is the prose channel's `Code`. It carries the same `origin` and the same `fragments`, so one `Part` type serves both channels. A fragment is a span of the section's prose, and a reference transcludes another section's prose. The channel a fragment belongs to is the field that holds it, never the fragment itself. Prose is always Markdown, so a `WovenProse` declares no `languageId`.

=>

export const WovenProseSchema = productNode('WovenProse', {
  origin: SectionIdSchema,
  fragments: Schema.Array(PartSchema),
})
export type WovenProse = typeof WovenProseSchema.Type

## A tangled file

A `File` is what a tangle section composes to: a file `path` paired with the `Code` bound to it. It is a value, not an effect — the tangler flattens the `code` across the corpus and performs the write, so building a `File` touches no disk.

=>

export const FileSchema = productNode('File', {
  path: Schema.String,
  code: CodeSchema,
})
export type File = typeof FileSchema.Type

## The module's product

`Product` is a module's whole composition — the graph the product pass produces for one `.loom`. A module has many roots, so a `Product` holds many nodes: its `code`, one `Code` per ordinary section, and its `files`, one `File` per tangle sink. The edges are the refs those nodes carry. A node no same-module ref folds in is a root; the rest are members, reached only through an edge.

The graph is what `compile` resolves. It reads a `Product`, takes the roots, and flattens each by following its edges across the corpus. So a `Product` is left unresolved here — it carries the whole graph, not the flattened roots — and resolving it is the next pass's work, not this structure's. Prose is no part of it: the narration is read from the parse, in document order, never composed here.

=>

export const ProductSchema = Schema.Struct({
  code: Schema.Array(CodeSchema),
  files: Schema.Array(FileSchema),
})
export type Product = typeof ProductSchema.Type

## What this module draws on

Effect's `Schema` defines the nodes, and `Effect` fills the health default. `Option` and `Array` read what an edge promises. `Health`, `okHealth`, and `Position` come from the foundation, giving each node its health and its source spans.

=>

import { Array, Effect, Option, Schema } from 'effect'
import { HealthSchema, okHealth, PositionSchema } from '#ast/LoomNode'

